<title>GERMANY: Early German harvest unlikely despite rains.</title>
<headline>Early German harvest unlikely despite rains.</headline>
<dateline>OFFENBACH 1997-06-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Rains in May boosted grains and oilseeds crops but an early harvest is unlikely because of a delayed spring, Germany's national meteorological office, DWD, said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;It's just been too cold for too long this year,&quot; a DWD source said.</p>
<p>More showers were forecast for the next seven days, when maximum daytime temperatures should be 22 degrees.</p>
<p>The precipitation should help the driest areas in northern Bavaria and in the south of former eastern Germany to catch up with the rest, DWD said.</p>
<p>Sugar beet and maize would also benefit from the rains.</p>
<p>DWD added that lower temperatures were reducing pest problems.</p>
<p>--Hamburg newsroom +49-40-41903275</p>
